<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Zaachila</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1521 CE (approx.) · capital Zaachila</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Zapotec kingdom of the Valley of Oaxaca after Monte Albán, whose kings later carried Zapotec power to Tehuantepec.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Zaachila, called Teozapotlan by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahuas</a>, emerged as the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotec-civilization">Zapotec</a> royal seat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-oaxaca">Valley of Oaxaca</a> in the Postclassic period, from around 1100, filling the gap left by the decline of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/monte-alban">Monte Albán</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its rulers held the highest prestige among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotec-civilization">Zapotec</a> lords, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mitla">Mitla</a> served as its religious centre. Zaachila was linked by marriage and rivalry to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixtecs">Mixtec</a> lords, who settled in parts of the valley. Two decorated tombs excavated in 1962 contained Mixtec-style gold, turquoise and polychrome pottery. In the late 15th century King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cocijoeza">Cocijoeza</a> resisted the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> and allied with it through marriage.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cocijoeza">Cocijoeza</a> moved the royal seat to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zapotec-kingdom-of-tehuantepec">Tehuantepec</a>, and Zaachila lost importance. The Spanish took control of the valley around 1521, and Zaachila became a colonial town.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zaachila.jpg" alt="Zaachila" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: El Ágora · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zaachila_Panorama.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1521 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zaachila</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cocijoeza</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zapotec religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Zapotec</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zapotec kingdom of Zaachila, Teozapotlan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1310&amp;focus=zaachila" aria-label="Zaachila on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2048 1548 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zaachila lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2368 1804L2369 1801M2368 1804L2321 1717M2368 1804L2203 1741"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2369 1801h0M2321 1717h0M2203 1741h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2368 1804h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2368 1804h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1310&amp;focus=zaachila">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
